A timestamp we cannot check is not a bad code signature.

MEASURED, three bundles in one container so the probe carried its own control, and all three came
back the same: MoneyMoney, iTerm and LibreOffice each gave -67061, kCMSSignerInvalidSignature. The
other two only run because they never check their own signature at startup. MoneyMoney does, and
showed its user that the application file seems to be damaged.

THE SIGNATURE WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. Traced end to end:

    CIDER_CMS verify signer=0x..da40 exit=ok verdict=1 (GoodSignature)
    CIDER_CMS unauth attrs (timestamp) signer=0x..7a40 rux=-67884, abandoning a GoodSignature
    CIDER_CMS VerifySignerInfo signer=0x..7a40 signature=-1 certificate=0
    CIDER_CSSTEP core threw MacOSError error=-67061

Three signers, two verify clean, and the third has a timestamp countersignature whose trust cannot
be evaluated. Its GoodSignature verdict left by goto loser, and CMSDecoderCopySignerStatus flattens
any nonzero into kCMSSignerInvalidSignature, which its own comment admits is an assumption.

TWO CHANGES, each defensible on its own terms.

cmssiginfo.c no longer discards a good signature because the unauthenticated attributes failed. A
timestamp says WHEN something was signed, not WHETHER. A BAD signature still fails: vs is untouched
and the SecCmsVSBadSignature path is unchanged.

StaticCode.cpp treats errSecTimestampNotTrusted from CMSDecoderCopySignerTimestampWithPolicy the
same as errSecTimestampMissing, which was already tolerated one line above. Nothing is claimed that
we do not have: mSigningTimestamp stays 0, so the code takes its own "no timestamp available"
branch. The surrounding code already takes this position for the cases Apple anticipated,
returning noErr for CSSMERR_TP_CERT_EXPIRED and CSSMERR_TP_CERT_NOT_VALID_YET so that a TSA chain
detail does not stop someone running an application.

AND WHY THE TIMESTAMP CANNOT BE CHECKED, which is the finding under all of this and is OURS:

    CIDER_TSA SecTrustEvaluate result=0 trustResult=0 cssmResultCode=0 (getCssm=0)

trustResult 0 is kSecTrustResultInvalid, which the header defines as "SecTrustEvaluate has not yet
been called" and which tsaSupport handles under an assert(false) marked should never happen.
SecTrustEvaluate returns SUCCESS while leaving that result, and exactly one code path can do it:
TRUSTD_XPC(sec_trust_evaluate) fails with errSecNotAvailable, and Apple's ramdisk fallback then
returns true having set only the leaf, in its own words to make it seem like we did a cert
evaluation. So every trust evaluation in this container is a lie its callers each mishandle
differently.

THE BUNDLE IS THEREFORE STILL NOT VALID, and that is said plainly rather than claimed otherwise: the
verdict moves from -67061 to -50, because validateDirectory asks the same broken SecTrustEvaluate
and gets the same kSecTrustResultInvalid. What these changes buy is a TRUE label instead of a false
one, and a chain with no remaining unknowns in it. The one thing left is a trust evaluation that
actually happens, which needs trustd to answer.

The step traces are what made this legible, and they are kept: mTrust after the CMS status, the
timestamp switch, each SecTrustSet call, and the trust result codesigning itself gets.
